  "scriptId": "tr-07-meetings-stations-timetables",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Train Meetings Between Stations and Timetables",
    "description": "Solve when and where trains meet, including delayed starts, distance differences at the meeting point, and after-meeting travel times.",
    "teachingApproach": "Move from simultaneous and delayed station starts to meeting-point distance differences, route-rate meetings, the square-root after-meeting relation, route reconstruction, timetable arithmetic, and a full after-meeting capstone.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 20, Problems on Trains. Approved scope: Teach station-to-station meeting problems using closing speed after accounting for any head start. Cover simultaneous starts, delayed starts, finding route length from the difference in distances travelled at meeting, and meeting times from full-route travel times. Teach the chapter's after-meeting relation: when two trains start simultaneously from opposite ends, meet, then take a and b hours to reach the far ends, their speed ratio is sqrt(b):sqrt(a). Use it to recover speeds and route length. Finish with timetable capstones that convert a start delay into distance already covered before closing begins."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Train-length questions zoom in on seconds. Station-meeting questions zoom out to whole journeys.\n\nThe engine is still relative speed — first account for any head start, then close the remaining route.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Two trains starting from opposite stations are just a large relative-speed problem. If the stations are 240 km apart and speeds are 60 and 40 km/hr, the gap closes at 100 km/hr.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Meeting time = 240/100 = 2.4 h = 2 h 24 min.\n\nHere train lengths are irrelevant because the question asks when their fronts meet, not when the trains completely cross each other.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
