    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A famous train pattern starts after the meeting. Two trains start simultaneously from opposite ends, meet, then train A takes 4 h to reach B while train B takes 9 h to reach A.\n\nThe speed ratio is not 9:4. It is the square-root ratio: A:B = √9:√4 = 3:2.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5_step": {
      "id": "teach_5_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Why? Before meeting, each train's distance is proportional to its speed. After meeting, each train covers the distance the other had already covered. Combining those two proportional relationships produces the square.\n\nMemory hook: after-meeting times 4 and 9 → speeds 3 and 2, reversed under square roots.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_5"
        }
      ]
    },
